Introduction
In
Part IV
, we extended the Interactive Supply and Demand Zone Manager beyond zone detection and lifecycle tracking by connecting validated supply and demand zones to a trading layer. The framework could now evaluate zone interactions and use them as part of an automated trading workflow.
After a restart of the Expert Advisor or the trading terminal, the zone engine in its previous form could rediscover rectangles on the chart but lost all runtime continuity: lifecycle status (Virgin/Broken/Inactive), interaction history and touch counts, scores and tiering, buy/sell trigger flags, blacklist entries, and the managed geometry of user-drawn zones. In practice this causes restored zones to behave as new ones — risks include duplicate or inappropriate trade entries, incorrect filtering, desynchronized visuals and logic, and the need for users to redraw or reconfigure manual boxes.
Part V remedies this by adding a persistence layer that records the logical state of the zone engine per trading context (symbol + timeframe). The persistence design intentionally separates runtime behavior from durable state: SZonePersist is a plain-data snapshot that captures identity, geometry, timing, scores/telemetry, lifecycle flags and user time anchors; CZone remains responsible for detection, lifecycle evaluation and trading. On OnInit(), the framework restores the saved zone properties and lifecycle flags, reconciles their chart objects, and applies the existing startup relevance checks. Persistence remains separate from detection and execution.
Note:
Part V supports zone-state restoration across normal shutdowns and restarts when a usable snapshot is available; it does not implement transactional crash-safe writes or restore the complete trading-engine state.
Architectural Focus of This Phase
The objective is to preserve the logical state of the zone engine independently from the lifetime of the EA process.
This phase therefore focuses on four areas:
Persisting zone state so active zones can be reconstructed after a restart.
Restoring lifecycle and interaction state rather than treating restored zones as newly detected structures.
Reconciling persisted state with chart objects, including restoring user-zone price boundaries and left time anchors while retaining the framework’s right-edge extension policy.
Registering user-drawn zones into the managed collection with an initial baseline score so they can be evaluated by the existing trading pipeline.
To achieve this, we introduce persistence as another layer around the existing zone engine, allowing the framework to retain its state while keeping detection, lifecycle management, and trading responsibilities intact.
Designing the Persistence Snapshot
Before implementing the save and restore workflow, the framework needs a representation of zone state that can exist independently of the runtime CZone object.
The persistence layer introduces SZonePersist, a plain-data structure that contains the information required to reconstruct a logical zone after the EA restarts. This separation is deliberate. The persistence module does not need to understand how CZone performs detection, lifecycle evaluation, trading, or chart rendering. It only needs to store the state required to rebuild the zone later.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| SZonePersist: Plain-data snapshot of ONE logical zone            |
//| (decoupled from the CZone runtime class so this module never     |
//|  needs to "understand" the lifecycle engine).                    |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
struct
SZonePersist
  {
//--- Identity / Provenance
string
name;
// Stable chart-object / zone identifier
int
zone_type;
// ENUM_ZONE_TYPE (Support/Resistance/Neutral)
int
zone_status;
// ENUM_ZONE_STATUS (Virgin/Broken/Inactive)
bool
is_user_zone;
// AUTO or USER provenance
//--- Geometry
double
top;
double
bottom;
//--- Timing
datetime
created;
// exact generation timestamp
datetime
last_touch_time;
// last price test interaction
datetime
broken_time;
// breakout confirmation stamp
//--- Score
int
zone_score;
// static structural weight score
double
current_zone_score;
// LIVE score used by trading gate
string
zone_tier;
// categorized tier label
//--- Telemetry
double
initial_volume_ratio;
double
departure_velocity;
int
touch_count;
int
bounce_count;
int
failure_count;
double
max_reaction_pips;
int
resurrection_count;
string
breakout_quality;
//--- Lifecycle flags (affect next state transition)
bool
is_pending;
bool
is_broken;
int
consecutive_closes_outside;
int
consecutive_rejection_closes;
bool
buy_triggered;
bool
sell_triggered;
//--- Part V Stored time anchors (USER left anchor and managed right edge)
datetime
time_left;
// OBJPROP_TIME 0 (left edge of the rectangle the user drew)
datetime
time_right;
// Stored right anchor (0 = unspecified; use current time on restoration)
};
The structure groups the state required to reconstruct a zone into four areas:
identity and provenance
,
geometry and timing,
score and telemetry
, and
lifecycle state
. This preserves not only where the zone is, but also the information accumulated throughout its lifetime. For user-drawn zones, the left time anchor and framework-assigned right anchor are stored. Registration and manual modification set the right edge to the current time, and runtime rendering continues extending it; the original finite drawing length is not preserved. Transient rendering properties are intentionally excluded. These belong to the chart layer and can be recreated from the restored logical state.
This keeps the boundary clear: SZonePersist stores what the zone is and what its current state is
, while CZone remains responsible for
runtime management
.
Establishing the Persistence Path
Before a zone can survive an EA or terminal restart, the framework first needs a well-defined location in which its state can be stored.
This is handled by SetupPersistencePath().
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Setup persistence folder and assign the state file path          |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
SetupPersistencePath(
void
)
  {
FolderCreate
(ZP_FOLDER,
FILE_COMMON
);
   g_persist.SetPath(PersistFilePath());
  }
The purpose of this function is deliberately narrow. It prepares the environment required by the persistence layer rather than performing any zone serialization itself. Keeping this responsibility separate means the rest of the framework does not need to know how the storage location is prepared.
This becomes important because persistence is not part of the zone-detection process. The detection engine continues to operate on its runtime collection of CZone objects, while the persistence layer provides a separate mechanism for making that runtime state durable.
Once the persistence path has been established, the framework needs to determine which persistence file belongs to the current chart context.
That responsibility is handled by PersistFilePath(). Snapshots are stored under Terminal\Common\Files\DynamicSR\V5. File identity uses only symbol and chart timeframe, so this version assumes one writer per symbol/timeframe in the shared storage location. Accounts, EA instances, and parameter sets are not isolated by the filename.
#define
ZP_FOLDER
"DynamicSR\\V5"
// Folder under Terminal/Common/Files
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Part V Logical-state persistence helpers                         |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
string
PersistFilePath(
void
)
  {
return
ZP_FOLDER +
"\\DSR5_"
+
_Symbol
+
"_"
+
IntegerToString
(
_Period
) +
".bin"
;
  }
Rather than treating persistence as one global store, the framework builds the file identity from the trading context in which the EA is running. This allows the saved state for one symbol and timeframe to remain distinct from another.
For example, the state associated with a zone engine running on one symbol and timeframe should not accidentally be loaded when the same EA is attached to a different chart context.
This gives the persistence layer two important properties:
A known storage location — established by SetupPersistencePath().
A context-specific persistence identity — produced by PersistFilePath().
At this point, no zone has been saved yet. The framework has simply established where persistent state belongs and how the correct snapshot is identified.
That separation is intentional. The next stage is to determine when the live zone state has changed enough to require persistence, and how an individual runtime zone is converted into a form that can be stored.
Detecting State Changes
With the persistence location established, the next problem is deciding when the runtime state actually needs to be written to storage.
The framework handles this through MarkPersistDirty().
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Mark persistence state for the next save cycle                   |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
MarkPersistDirty(
void
)
  {
   g_persist_dirty =
true
;
  }
Rather than writing the entire zone state to disk whenever any part of the EA executes, the framework marks the persistence state as dirty when a change occurs that should eventually be preserved.
This creates a simple distinction between two conditions:
Clean — the persisted snapshot already represents the current state.
Dirty — the runtime state has changed, and the stored snapshot may no longer be current.
The function itself does not perform the save. It simply records that persistence work is required. That separation is useful because zone state can change as part of normal runtime processing. A zone may progress through its lifecycle, accumulate interactions, change its score, or otherwise acquire information that must survive a restart. The component detecting that change does not need to perform file operations immediately.
Instead, it signals the persistence layer.
The next step is therefore to determine what information from the runtime CZone object should actually be preserved.
That conversion is handled by PersistZoneToRecord().
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Copy runtime zone state into a persistence record                |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
PersistZoneToRecord(
const
CZone *z, SZonePersist &r)
  {
   r.name                  = z.name;
   r.zone_type             = (
int
)z.type;
   r.zone_status           = (
int
)z.status;
   r.is_user_zone          = z.is_user_zone;
   r.top                   = z.top;
   r.bottom                = z.bottom;
   r.created               = z.created;
   r.last_touch_time       = z.last_touch_time;
   r.broken_time           = z.broken_time;
   r.zone_score            = z.zone_score;
   r.current_zone_score    = z.Current_zone_score;
   r.zone_tier             = z.zone_tier;
   r.initial_volume_ratio  = z.initial_volume_ratio;
   r.departure_velocity    = z.departure_velocity;
   r.touch_count           = z.touch_count;
   r.bounce_count          = z.bounce_count;
   r.failure_count         = z.failure_count;
   r.max_reaction_pips     = z.max_reaction_pips;
   r.resurrection_count    = z.resurrection_count;
   r.breakout_quality      = z.breakout_quality;
   r.is_pending            = z.is_pending;
   r.is_broken             = z.is_broken;
   r.consecutive_closes_outside     = z.consecutive_closes_outside;
   r.consecutive_rejection_closes   = z.consecutive_rejection_closes;
   r.buy_triggered         = z.buy_triggered;
   r.sell_triggered        = z.sell_triggered;
   r.time_left             = z.time_left;
// Part V real time anchors
r.time_right            = z.time_right;
  }
Here, the framework moves from change detection to state representation.
A live CZone contains runtime information used by the engine, but the persistence layer should not simply attempt to store the entire runtime object as-is. Instead, the relevant state is mapped into a persistence record containing the information required to reconstruct the zone later.
This creates a boundary between the two representations:
Runtime CZone → Persistence Record.
The runtime object remains responsible for zone behavior, while the persistence record provides a stable representation of the information that needs to survive beyond the lifetime of the EA instance.
With changes now identifiable and individual zones convertible into persistence records, the framework has the pieces required to construct a complete snapshot.
Building and Saving the Zone Snapshot
Once the framework knows that persistence is required and can convert an individual CZone into a persistence record, it can construct a complete representation of the current zone engine state.
This is handled by SavePersistState().
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Save the current zone and blacklist state                        |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
SavePersistState(
void
)
  {
   SZonePersist records[];
int
n = active_zones.Total();
if
(n <=
0
)
ArrayResize
(records,
0
);
else
{
ArrayResize
(records, n);
int
fill =
0
;
for
(
int
i =
0
; i < n; i++)
        {
         CZone *z = (CZone*)active_zones.At(i);
if
(z ==
NULL
)
continue
;
         PersistZoneToRecord(z, records[fill]);
         fill++;
        }
ArrayResize
(records, fill);
     }
double
blk[];
int
bc = blacklist.Total();
if
(bc <=
0
)
ArrayResize
(blk,
0
);
else
{
ArrayResize
(blk, bc);
for
(
int
i =
0
; i < bc; i++)
         blk[i] =
StringToDouble
(blacklist.At(i));
     }

   g_persist_dirty = !g_persist.SaveSnapshot(records, blk);
  }
SavePersistState() operates at the level of the zone collection, rather than an individual zone. It takes the current runtime state maintained by the framework and builds the set of persistence records that will represent that state across a restart. The dirty flag is cleared only when SaveSnapshot() returns true. When SaveSnapshot() reports failure, the dirty flag remains set for another attempt, including during startup.
The process can therefore be viewed as:
Runtime zone collection → persistence records → snapshot.
This is where the earlier separation between runtime objects and persistence data becomes useful. The persistence routine does not need to reproduce the internal behavior of a CZone; it only needs to gather the state that the framework has explicitly identified as durable.
Once the snapshot has been assembled, the actual storage operation is delegated to the persistence component.
That responsibility belongs to CZonePersistence::SaveSnapshot().
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CZonePersistence::SaveSnapshot                                   |
//| Binary layout:                                                   |
//| Header  : MAGIC(int) | FORMAT_VERSION(int)                       |
//| Zones   : count(int) { SZonePersist record }*count               |
//| Blacklist: count(int) | double price*count                       |
//| FileFlush() flushes buffered writes; this is not an atomic save. |
//| This represents CURRENT logical state (deleted zones ARE NOT     |
//| present here - the caller only feeds what is logically alive).   |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
CZonePersistence::SaveSnapshot(SZonePersist &zones[],
double
&blacklist_prices[])
  {
if
(m_path ==
""
)
return
false
;
int
h =
FileOpen
(m_path,
FILE_WRITE
|
FILE_BIN
|
FILE_COMMON
);
if
(h ==
INVALID_HANDLE
)
     {
PrintFormat
(
"[Part V][PERSIST] Save failed. FileOpen error: %d"
,
GetLastError
());
return
false
;
     }
//--- Header
FileWriteInteger
(h,(
int
)ZP_MAGIC,
INT_VALUE
);
FileWriteInteger
(h,ZP_FORMAT_VERSION,
INT_VALUE
);
//--- Zones
int
zc =
ArraySize
(zones);
FileWriteInteger
(h,zc,
INT_VALUE
);
for
(
int
i=
0
;i<zc;i++)
      WriteZone(h,zones[i]);
//--- Blacklist
int
bc =
ArraySize
(blacklist_prices);
FileWriteInteger
(h,bc,
INT_VALUE
