for
(
int
i=
0
;i<bc;i++)
FileWriteDouble
(h,blacklist_prices[i]);
FileFlush
(h);
// flush buffered writes
FileClose
(h);
return
true
;
  }
This function forms the boundary between the zone engine and the storage mechanism.
The zone manager knows what state needs to be preserved. CZonePersistence knows how that state is written to persistent storage. This keeps file-handling details out of the zone-management logic and allows the persistence mechanism to remain independent of zone detection and lifecycle processing.
Architectural note: durability versus crash-safety
FileFlush() flushes buffered file writes, but it does not make replacement of the snapshot atomic. SaveSnapshot() currently writes a single state file directly, with a magic marker and format version for validation, but without a backup copy, checksum, or temporary-file swap.
Part V preserves state across normal EA and terminal restarts; it does not provide transactional crash recovery
.
A crash or termination during writing can leave an incomplete snapshot. The loader rejects files that fail its format, completeness, or value checks, and startup proceeds without restoring that snapshot. Without a checksum, these checks do not detect every possible form of corruption.
The same distinction applies to the dirty-state mechanism: changes are persisted during the normal runtime cycle and are also handled during graceful deinitialization, but a hard termination can occur before the latest change reaches storage.
This is an intentional scope boundary for Part V. The persistence layer supports normal restart restoration; crash-safe transactional persistence would require additional mechanisms such as atomic replacement or backup snapshots.
Loading the Persisted Snapshot
Saving the snapshot gives the framework a durable representation of its zone state, but persistence is only useful if that information can be recovered when the EA starts again.
The restoration process begins with LoadPersistState().
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Restore persisted zones and blacklist state                      |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
LoadPersistState(
void
)
  {
   active_zones.Clear();
   blacklist.Clear();

   SZonePersist records[];
double
blk[];
int
version
= g_persist.LoadSnapshot(records, blk);
if
(
version
<
0
)
     {
PrintFormat
(
"[Part V][PERSIST] No usable snapshot (code %d). Starting fresh."
,
version
);
return
;
     }
int
bc =
ArraySize
(blk);
for
(
int
i =
0
; i < bc; i++)
      blacklist.Add(
DoubleToString
(blk[i],
_Digits
));
int
n =
ArraySize
(records);
for
(
int
i =
0
; i < n; i++)
     {
      CZone *z = RebuildZoneFromRecord(records[i]);
if
(z !=
NULL
)
         active_zones.Add(z);
     }
PrintFormat
(
"[Part V][PERSIST] Restored %d zones / %d blacklist entries (format v%d)."
,
               active_zones.Total(), blacklist.Total(),
version
);
  }
Unlike the saving process, which starts with live runtime objects, loading starts with the persisted representation. The function requests the previously stored snapshot and uses it as the source for rebuilding the framework's logical state.
The actual file-reading and deserialization work remains inside the persistence component, through CZonePersistence::LoadSnapshot().
