The responsibility of this function is to compare the restored zone state with the objects currently present on the chart and bring the visual layer back into agreement with the runtime state.
This is particularly important across terminal and EA restarts. The CZone object reconstructed from persistence exists in memory, while its corresponding rectangle may no longer exist on the chart. In that situation, reconciliation recreates the missing chart representation using the persisted zone information.
For user-drawn zones, reconciliation restores the saved price boundaries and left time anchor. The right edge remains framework-managed and continues extending during runtime. A chart object represents the zone; it is not the source of the zone's lifecycle state. The persisted runtime state remains authoritative, while ReconcileChartObjects() restores the visual representation needed to reflect that state.
This also allows the framework to handle cases where the chart and persistence state are not initially identical. An object may be missing, already present, or require its visual properties to be synchronized with the restored zone state.
The startup sequence can therefore be understood as two related restorations:
Logical restoration
Persistent snapshot → CZone
Visual reconciliation
CZone → chart object
This separation prevents the chart from becoming a second, competing source of truth for zone state.
Restored zones have their saved lifecycle state and chart representation available again. Those retained by the subsequent startup relevance check then enter normal runtime processing.
